mix"}, {"id": "1cea37b7-6c4b-4525-b02e-2040b36e216d", "type": "concept", "title": "Antitheatrical criticism"}], "description": "Gist: The plays emerged from a commercial theater world with companies, audiences, rival playwrights, and civic suspicion."}, {"id": "ccf8e47d-b8ca-4a0f-bce6-5ded994b68f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading historically", "children": [{"id": "b24c4dcd-d407-4f24-8c11-e6181ef7cc71", "type": "concept", "title": "Period terms"}, {"id": "5eaefa86-85b5-46d8-88e0-df83217aacbb", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronology"}, {"id": "e70c5bdd-96bb-4ffe-861b-2445e67b1e52", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextual evidence"}, {"id": "aa395ec3-c2fe-4273-8dce-7ba7966d7aeb", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding presentism"}], "description": "Gist: Historical context becomes a tool for sharper interpretation of language, plot, and performance."}]}, {"id": "c585b772-db9f-4905-bca3-e9c290b7e8d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Shakespearean Language, Rhetoric, and Verse", "description": "Build skill with Shakespeare's vocabulary, syntax, rhetoric, blank verse, prose, and dramatic speech.", "children": [{"id": "553eb179-02bd-4115-8737-f3dc22f3e59b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vocabulary and syntax", "children": [{"id": "cf9331af-927a-47be-98af-bb95ddc24d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Word shifts"}, {"id": "6740862d-9797-4b2f-a31d-70a78dadc9f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Inverted syntax"}, {"id": "0b359417-9de1-4269-b584-aa2b48c048cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Contractions"}, {"id": "598450a3-7c09-474c-8e37-b4818c0fcacc", "type": "concept", "title": "False friends"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn to slow down and make Shakespeare's language legible through grammar, glossing, and paraphrase."}, {"id": "f1cc125c-f92a-4538-a52f-5fe5183c3cbd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetorical craft", "children": [{"id": "9ba34926-5cc5-4249-a8c1-a775619556d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Antithesis"}, {"id": "e87be880-48db-4d01-8609-e59bc27e1d43", "type": "concept", "title": "Anaphora"}, {"id": "9fc2ec47-d7fb-4785-a7af-2cc5e056064b", "type": "concept", "title": "Puns"}, {"id": "7c855c18-6042-4906-a7e8-5b577c54f235", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphor"}], "description": "Gist: Characters think, persuade, evade, and reveal themselves through rhetorical patterning."}, {"id": "128b6bd4-500f-44c6-a734-d89fa758ca01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Verse and prose", "children": [{"id": "f06d7e9e-9546-4c2c-a257-b081878a84d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Iambic pentameter"}, {"id": "62db926c-e632-4cb8-86ef-520fbb08123c", "type": "concept", "title": "Blank verse"}, {"id": "ddf0029e-3bf2-4db4-a0b6-f05720f8f2bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared lines"}, {"id": "ddc7d4ab-1b0f-4889-8271-3ec973dbecd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Prose scenes"}], "description": "Gist: Form choices shape class, intimacy, pressure, performance rhythm, and dramatic change."}]}, {"id": "95018d34-e7b6-4c6a-b1c3-983beaeb7f51", "type": "topic", "title": "Performance, Stagecraft, and Theatrical Institutions", "description": "Read plays as scripts for embodied performance shaped by actors, audiences, playhouses, companies, and staging conventions.", "children": [{"id": "50971698-0b38-48f8-b37a-4bfe14da47a3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Plays as scripts", "children": [{"id": "98e22b2a-43f6-4eb6-9c2a-ab9bc1e159b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Blocking"}, {"id": "b6cd0d0c-9e43-4cb0-b82d-23b374668a0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture"}, {"id": "2ba85f42-6011-4480-bfc3-934ef088912b", "type": "concept", "title": "Pacing"}, {"id": "dc3303e6-8dac-470e-9544-bddb82b0a016", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience address"}], "description": "Gist: A play text is a performance score that must be activated by bodies, voices, space, and timing."}, {"id": "80540903-3545-44b2-a5f5-e08a2bea2961", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early modern staging", "children": [{"id": "278f4a73-08f7-4e63-a546-40ad91ab6746", "type": "concept", "title": "Bare stage"}, {"id": "555bda3d-ba93-4767-8ab7-77252fa63bf6", "type": "concept", "title": "Discovery space"}, {"id": "5d8e6b2e-4417-4e0f-94b1-0e79b30dc756", "type": "concept", "title": "Props"}, {"id": "fa64ea0b-d566-45d0-b451-5d09abfaee89", "type": "concept", "title": "Doubling"}], "description": "Gist: Students study how limited scenery and shared conventions made language, costume, and entrances do major work."}, {"id": "70238136-45dc-4a02-a559-48bb95660d33", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Performance interpretation", "children": [{"id": "62887630-b492-47a4-b9bc-f15eaf1aa2f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Casting"}, {"id": "4f75ac3d-c5d1-4db1-b53a-d12edf56acc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Cuts"}, {"id": "078767a6-7829-4fc2-86ea-00a3828ddd0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Tone"}, {"id": "38326131-498e-41c7-82a6-d9ae1ea4a9ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Design concept"}], "description": "Gist: Different staging choices create different arguments about the same scene."}]}, {"id": "bdc10900-487b-41dd-8127-89b8b63d0c33", "type": "topic", "title": "Comedy, Festivity, and Social Order", "description": "Study comic form through disguise, courtship, wit, festivity, misrule, and the restoration or questioning of social order.", "children": [{"id": "caf0cc45-ddce-48f8-9363-4011346aaa8c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comic structures", "children": [{"id": "ff1170b6-7e71-4aa8-a174-054d62d87519", "type": "concept", "title": "Mistaken identity"}, {"id": "6a85e3d0-76d1-4771-acad-7af28b51c9c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Courtship plots"}, {"id": "cfc5c43d-4850-437a-a702-b51608374b2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Festive misrule"}, {"id": "84d055b0-3c1b-4f00-a5cd-f17caf16c1d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Comic resolution"}], "description": "Gist: Comedy often moves through confusion, desire, disguise, and social repair."}, {"id": "1af2817a-3c0c-4121-9d78-fff0b32e8ce9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Wit and social play", "children": [{"id": "e985ea5a-eeef-4c06-acbf-233f465138f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Banter"}, {"id": "96112b77-e9fc-4dec-8fc7-5ed5cddc70b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Wordplay"}, {"id": "704ed7b9-7a37-4e1e-824f-fabff152b487", "type": "concept", "title": "Eavesdropping"}, {"id": "b1c959b4-64be-4b8d-8b12-52f1ce093602", "type": "concept", "title": "Mockery"}], "description": "Gist: Comic language tests intelligence, power, intimacy, and belonging."}, {"id": "11fbc269-9f8b-4045-b57a-a48c279b4c93", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Uneasy endings", "children": [{"id": "76b9fc96-81d7-4fae-bb26-d9c49b01d453", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage closure"}, {"id": "74b299e7-7b47-4c17-a021-43f2abf4310c", "type": "concept", "title": "Class hierarchy"}, {"id": "7b40d5e9-8b04-4372-8709-6414e2559b5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender pressure"}, {"id": "e8364a61-02e5-499a-9126-a9f43bfaf9f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Exclusion"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn to ask what comedy repairs and what tensions remain unresolved."}]}, {"id": "6b0efe1e-becb-4ce3-b743-890f54847a53", "type": "topic", "title": "Tragedy, Character, and Catastrophe", "description": "Analyze tragic form, agency, suffering, recognition, political breakdown, and the making of dramatic interiority.", "children": [{"id": "c75276ed-1d83-430e-b600-631e3003144f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tragic form", "children": [{"id": "32328a2f-0721-42ae-ae1b-b64c938fe061", "type": "concept", "title": "Hamartia debates"}, {"id": "37776f67-ed17-4fd9-919c-e05f051d2792", "type": "concept", "title": "Recognition"}, {"id": "61f49ab7-0401-464c-93dd-7b2321ad6448", "type": "concept", "title": "Reversal"}, {"id": "1b1f8a2f-4504-43d8-80bf-6402c875618e", "type": "concept", "title": "Catastrophe"}], "description": "Gist: Tragedy turns choice, chance, error, ambition, and social pressure into irreversible crisis."}, {"id": "743fe95e-fbdd-4faa-a345-e98330d90a24", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interior life and soliloquy", "children": [{"id": "66e50c28-3d9d-448e-b7fb-3782310bf730", "type": "concept", "title": "Aside"}, {"id": "2dad8070-0094-4977-b67f-b7311a86474c", "type": "concept", "title": "Soliloquy"}, {"id": "bdb22e81-04be-4bff-957e-a04bb09a872b", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-address"}, {"id": "5db3f164-1cb8-42f5-aeb6-e9b8f2c2c501", "type": "concept", "title": "Dramatic irony"}], "description": "Gist: Soliloquies let audiences encounter thought as performance, self-division, and rhetorical action."}, {"id": "9ac74150-4c2d-4c47-9bef-206e23447827", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of suffering", "children": [{"id": "dd424747-68a9-4458-b411-0e28778633b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Violence"}, {"id": "41fd2921-a485-42f4-a061-284e4d1cc181", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsibility"}, {"id": "b98f6040-0535-4798-a40e-c3390923471f", "type": "concept", "title": "Spectacle"}, {"id": "4ae16931-2eca-4497-a1a8-8bcbb7fb4030", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral ambiguity"}], "description": "Gist: Students examine how tragedy makes audiences judge, pity, fear, and question justice."}]}, {"id": "48e6632a-a90c-4024-8d12-492c237416d9", "type": "topic", "title": "History Plays, Sovereignty, and Political Theology", "description": "Examine Shakespeare's histories as meditations on monarchy, legitimacy, rebellion, providence, nation, and public memory.", "children": [{"id": "9b1a5eaa-1f14-4d8c-b823-f43362c91d0f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kingship and legitimacy", "children": [{"id": "9a3dcc21-0c7d-429d-8ff3-14c6efe8f0a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Succession"}, {"id": "ea5f4e2d-a664-42fa-8b0d-930daf40b587", "type": "concept", "title": "Usurpation"}, {"id": "96045780-9739-4ba3-a3e9-3a58085672b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Divine right"}, {"id": "c7229290-e90a-431c-94d7-7227be7ec7d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent"}], "description": "Gist: The histories ask what makes rule lawful, persuasive, fragile, or violent."}, {"id": "0261fa9f-1edf-428c-8a63-ec76ecbdd947", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nation and memory", "children": [{"id": "b4fd0c2f-f5bc-4b24-ab95-9da5e339281e", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronicle sources"}, {"id": "4ac0c355-60ab-41db-996f-e752896b6951", "type": "concept", "title": "Englishness"}, {"id": "82399fe0-88a7-4a63-a6ef-55fc4c43fafa", "type": "concept", "title": "War memory"}, {"id": "bd92cffb-f9f0-46d1-a692-06869f520c33", "type": "concept", "title": "Public ceremony"}], "description": "Gist: Historical drama shapes collective memory while exposing the theater of political mythmaking."}, {"id": "b8cbf463-f2bb-4621-b593-de2f43ff1619", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political theology", "children": [{"id": "957ac749-bf13-41af-9027-cd3ea93c5d26", "type": "concept", "title": "Providence"}, {"id": "7b5f6c55-c3e8-4329-82f6-ad5bbfaefed8", "type": "concept", "title": "Oaths"}, {"id": "2bc77d99-5f5e-4b0f-bbf9-37a8857ded0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Martyr imagery"}, {"id": "576c9625-be87-476b-8ed4-1ea243f91a35", "type": "concept", "title": "Ceremony"}], "description": "Gist: Students trace how sacred language and state power interact on stage."}]}, {"id": "1b3ef3fa-ef7a-41f3-951e-5b29c5a57b7f", "type": "topic", "title": "Romance and the Late Plays", "description": "Explore late Shakespeare through wonder, loss, reconciliation, family rupture, forgiveness, theatrical magic, and generic mixture.", "children": [{"id": "81527978-123b-470f-a744-fb80f227855a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genre mixture", "children": [{"id": "39f28035-ca5c-4ed0-91cb-9f7660dc5ccf", "type": "concept", "title": "Tragicomic structure"}, {"id": "f7eef24d-24e2-4189-970d-332f8236d53e", "type": "concept", "title": "Recognition scenes"}, {"id": "033afe54-df34-4df6-a915-836d6035243e", "type": "concept", "title": "Lost children"}, {"id": "6ee13aac-0714-4fb8-90c9-be9f68bba3d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastoral movement"}], "description": "Gist: The late plays combine tragedy, comedy, pastoral, miracle, music, and spectacle."}, {"id": "aed4dbdd-86a1-4faf-9c73-f974325eca58", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Time, loss, and restoration", "children": [{"id": "5ebc323b-1bac-4a30-80ca-4ddf81a793c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Forgiveness"}, {"id": "88226ebe-2423-49f8-a87b-d94811a942b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Repentance"}, {"id": "aa3e6c94-73c2-44fc-98c6-6acce16c1cc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Reunion"}, {"id": "9706b834-c473-452b-adcb-0f7618b6ba69", "type": "concept", "title": "Resurrection motifs"}], "description": "Gist: Romance asks whether time can heal rupture, guilt, exile, and family damage."}, {"id": "289bfc15-184b-4533-a83e-f6d91dbbbea5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theatrical wonder", "children": [{"id": "ff484c5c-e022-49cd-bc36-5b9ded34a6bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Masque"}, {"id": "4eb39cf3-5983-4b96-ad05-766e4cf65c0b", "type": "concept", "title": "Music"}, {"id": "796833c0-7e84-49a2-904a-8d6847cec106", "type": "concept", "title": "Stage magic"}, {"id": "6546d886-1478-4dc1-bed6-367ea740fd18", "type": "concept", "title": "Metatheater"}], "description": "Gist: Students study how romance foregrounds theater's power to create belief."}]}, {"id": "ed4d0e49-1721-4311-a6f4-93ade9f1f4d4", "type": "topic", "title": "Sonnets, Lyric Poetry, and Metaphysical Contexts", "description": "Read Shakespeare's sonnets and lyric forms through address, desire, time, beauty, reproduction, form, and poetic argument.", "children": [{"id": "f5f4859d-abcd-4a79-a078-182f3f1cfd2c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sonnet form", "children": [{"id": "7cd26668-36cb-46fb-8f02-983149835572", "type": "concept", "title": "Quatrains"}, {"id": "ae78e7eb-776e-4725-aa68-722b0cae3de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Couplet"}, {"id": "7b6f3a7c-3893-4973-809d-1f6a8abce6da", "type": "concept", "title": "Volta"}, {"id": "2f9516ca-7ca1-4b2d-a332-984a635e6f90", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhyme scheme"}], "description": "Gist: The sonnet teaches compressed argument through line, turn, rhyme, and sequence."}, {"id": "94e3532a-9648-4877-ace7-d9b620c3dccb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Desire, time, and address", "children": [{"id": "e186e061-73bc-4349-974d-3c8a8fe868b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Fair youth"}, {"id": "f6b08704-4b0d-415b-9c91-cf43684fe644", "type": "concept", "title": "Dark lady"}, {"id": "e1e96b5a-de5c-4e87-8e63-92ec44bfdbc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Immortality claims"}, {"id": "6e1c0197-a915-4e3e-9ced-47d4e6220740", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutability"}], "description": "Gist: Students examine lyric voice as a scene of persuasion, praise, anxiety, and self-display."}, {"id": "aa597774-a5bc-4879-956a-ed30ab3e4023", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry in context", "children": [{"id": "2c7a39c8-5264-4e96-a54b-a91d47c89d71", "type": "concept", "title": "Petrarchism"}, {"id": "4dfa6a60-c3b5-4558-92c0-fbbff36c7fab", "type": "concept", "title": "Dedication"}, {"id": "4b0b8c10-592e-48ab-be5f-585569cc47f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Print circulation"}, {"id": "17beecb0-1656-4b25-8b0e-123277542aa8", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphysical wit"}], "description": "Gist: Shakespeare's poems belong to wider early modern lyric and patronage cultures."}]}, {"id": "bdc3a6fb-7775-429a-ab8b-a10c10ecd611", "type": "topic", "title": "Sources, Intertextuality, and Classical Inheritance", "description": "Trace how Shakespeare transforms chronicles, novelle, classical texts, biblical language, and theatrical precedents.", "children": [{"id": "cb99d1a5-9fb1-410b-bff9-11ec1c982312", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Source transformation", "children": [{"id": "204dff17-8f2b-433e-9c9b-8fd0483cd8fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Holinshed"}, {"id": "959e5e43-b51b-43e1-9724-df0109c46689", "type": "concept", "title": "Plutarch"}, {"id": "459efbc2-f650-4d11-b6a8-4b7cf165ec31", "type": "concept", "title": "Ovid"}, {"id": "afd68016-fa3a-49f9-b103-71f041e745c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Italian novelle"}], "description": "Gist: Shakespeare's artistry often lies in what he changes, compresses, expands, or reassigns."}, {"id": "caf3e3d3-92c2-46e8-8d23-cecb142fc24f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical and biblical echoes", "children": [{"id": "63c2d958-15cd-43ff-bd7c-4c5913f0f5bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth"}, {"id": "6cbcc08c-5b66-4116-bd15-3211bda549b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Allusion"}, {"id": "97f14a22-d2b0-4cdf-91a0-9a895bb4cc9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Typology"}, {"id": "3d110775-57b3-41a5-a0a3-8dc28d9e6205", "type": "concept", "title": "Exempla"}], "description": "Gist: Inherited stories and phrases become dramatic tools for authority, irony, and recognition."}, {"id": "22784c72-006c-4072-8ffc-8b6c535087fb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intertheatricality", "children": [{"id": "611a7c40-8d07-47bb-9add-8b50de422e84", "type": "concept", "title": "Marlowe"}, {"id": "d03c6c6d-f032-4e46-8be3-feca294d231e", "type": "concept", "title": "Jonson"}, {"id": "db5048bf-fa3a-4cfa-bdc4-94e381677627", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenge tragedy"}, {"id": "4cd1872d-bad1-4a2a-a257-0817537e4389", "type": "concept", "title": "City comedy"}], "description": "Gist: The plays respond to other playwrights, genres, companies, and audience expectations."}]}, {"id": "baef6a9e-922f-4ff8-b9a2-3ce33a08652f", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Sexuality, Household, and Embodiment", "description": "Analyze how Shakespeare stages gender roles, sexuality, marriage, inheritance, the household, and bodily vulnerability.", "children": [{"id": "2d631852-a629-4209-9ada-4400a2654a67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender performance", "children": [{"id": "9199e06b-dfdd-45a1-87b7-60bc62f6448e", "type": "concept", "title": "Boy actors"}, {"id": "033df855-6c3d-46ba-9e51-6ee8cc20ae67", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-dressing"}, {"id": "9b6a8a6f-4a4a-4adb-a847-e2072011123a", "type": "concept", "title": "Masculinity"}, {"id": "09578073-f76f-4ca9-8bef-24a1a8de13b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Femininity"}], "description": "Gist: Shakespearean gender is staged through role, costume, language, desire, and household authority."}, {"id": "5f0accaa-91cd-40c3-ad49-d2fcb63cb9f0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marriage and household politics", "children": [{"id": "cac5138a-c92b-4b85-8907-dc436ff0e3fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Patriarchy"}, {"id": "76ac0f53-5977-4b39-909c-b5e977cf52be", "type": "concept", "title": "Dowry"}, {"id": "4e3ec4fb-3aae-4a61-9bae-07c4189e8411", "type": "concept", "title": "Obedience"}, {"id": "8347ff8e-c7be-4177-a079-d6a88bff84a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent"}], "description": "Gist: Domestic plots often expose property, inheritance, obedience, intimacy, and coercion."}, {"id": "aabfc91c-55c7-40fd-adce-f587b45fae66", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bodies on stage", "children": [{"id": "c2ba9774-96c7-4963-9068-6d6af518d9d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Madness"}, {"id": "1aee2e6d-fcae-4dab-bee8-2edc63deb8df", "type": "concept", "title": "Pregnancy anxieties"}, {"id": "932506ee-0195-4cea-98bf-32c76db3afae", "type": "concept", "title": "Blood"}, {"id": "b8deae9f-e67d-4faa-b3a0-1d60c60064d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Touch"}], "description": "Gist: Embodiment matters through illness, wounds, sleep, desire, age, and vulnerability."}]}, {"id": "e6f4bfa0-26c6-45a3-abaa-f38304fdff11", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Empire, Encounter, and the Wider World", "description": "Study early modern race-making, religion, trade, empire, travel, and cultural encounter in Shakespearean drama.", "children": [{"id": "e54fc542-b55d-4d02-b76d-9360e3cbf672", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early modern race-making", "children": [{"id": "c7ab88c7-68cb-468e-aab5-34805573aa92", "type": "concept", "title": "Moors"}, {"id": "71a43428-eb91-4b23-9045-6d7090c26f5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion"}, {"id": "2e963330-9ac6-46ec-86c8-f37bdfe1bda4", "type": "concept", "title": "Blackness"}, {"id": "8a13ad02-c218-4bd8-98f0-979f6b6d42a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Strangerhood"}], "description": "Gist: Students study race as an emerging historical formation tied to religion, color, geography, and status."}, {"id": "78ed4065-3718-4c0f-84d9-29421bf82b7a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire and encounter", "children": [{"id": "ccb66092-000d-4e79-9bae-44180e502a47", "type": "concept", "title": "The Mediterranean"}, {"id": "702e6070-4b34-48d6-874c-c00089194504", "type": "concept", "title": "The Atlantic"}, {"id": "c2599bc9-6853-45e3-881e-bbb08b40c02d", "type": "concept", "title": "New World discourse"}, {"id": "746daeb2-cfae-45b3-811a-bd7861df9f21", "type": "concept", "title": "Mercantile networks"}], "description": "Gist: Shakespeare's plays register travel, trade, colonization, and the unsettling of European perspective."}, {"id": "859f955c-2ca6-4207-b056-212b4945a842", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modern critical stakes", "children": [{"id": "61209d39-8452-4616-bccd-29d7234df270", "type": "concept", "title": "Representation"}, {"id": "7c8f29da-b7b7-4e7f-8065-6389666c02bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype"}, {"id": "b8909d7c-9020-40db-aca9-652360aa8a71", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance history"}, {"id": "39c059ca-e003-4cc8-a7ea-160c4d5b37ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical race approaches"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn to connect early modern materials with careful contemporary critical vocabulary."}]}, {"id": "c8ad66dc-1f1d-45c6-972f-7a7faec788ef", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Reformation, Conscience, and Belief", "description": "Understand how Reformation conflict, conscience, ritual, providence, and sacred language shape Shakespearean drama.", "children": [{"id": "e6c282f5-f67f-443b-9cee-13ac5279058c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reformation context", "children": [{"id": "e2a7d8bc-d1cf-4a16-8407-558527355d61", "type": "concept", "title": "Protestantism"}, {"id": "d1d94a96-0689-4764-88e2-c8b3342d6e22", "type": "concept", "title": "Catholic memory"}, {"id": "52510d25-d2dd-4d40-b7c3-e4f63e422e87", "type": "concept", "title": "Liturgy"}, {"id": "8642b6fb-77d2-47a9-8646-3ba049276614", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconoclasm"}], "description": "Gist: Shakespeare wrote in a culture shaped by religious conflict, censorship, and ritual change."}, {"id": "8d580db7-d891-44f1-8bda-1b8a1dfae707", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conscience and inwardness", "children": [{"id": "e36fec3d-89f5-472e-91c5-61a7f481797a", "type": "concept", "title": "Confession"}, {"id": "36b2f85a-d26d-47b5-88f2-07685156071f", "type": "concept", "title": "Providence"}, {"id": "a618f25b-e5ea-46d8-b787-6620408788b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Doubt"}, {"id": "1d987490-dc43-4f01-9b93-2c6d50342b96", "type": "concept", "title": "Damnation"}], "description": "Gist: Religious language shapes questions of guilt, vocation, judgment, and interior conflict."}, {"id": "25e7ce80-6a52-48a5-aa3c-1bd278a91d18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sacred language on stage", "children": [{"id": "7e652088-731a-44d9-9e5e-8572ba153c6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Oaths"}, {"id": "36f3f167-6a92-427a-ae42-b92742537372", "type": "concept", "title": "Prayer"}, {"id": "b5c47859-8c01-418e-ad7c-7267f35f30c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Ghosts"}, {"id": "64290e97-11e2-4b16-9fd9-6f36f15d1b5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Afterlife"}], "description": "Gist: The plays reuse biblical, liturgical, and theological language for dramatic effect."}]}, {"id": "826bcd7c-cf9c-402a-989e-181b38f172c0", "type": "topic", "title": "Textual Transmission, Editing, Print, and Censorship", "description": "Introduce quartos, folios, textual variants, editorial practice, censorship, authorship, and material book history.", "children": [{"id": "8413e571-90c1-42be-92fe-4d4fe87956a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Quartos and folios", "children": [{"id": "35963275-9ae6-4128-b83e-21ffd1049420", "type": "concept", "title": "Bad quarto debates"}, {"id": "000cfac8-5881-4d97-bf72-9535c147961d", "type": "concept", "title": "First Folio"}, {"id": "31af4807-d59e-4d59-84b6-43f9ac30588d", "type": "concept", "title": "Variant passages"}, {"id": "8cb1b212-2c0d-4905-bdb3-58fe49d106db", "type": "concept", "title": "Copy text"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn why Shakespeare's texts exist in multiple early editions and why that matters."}, {"id": "a2c7ea04-19db-4839-90dc-d48d27fc4046", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Editing Shakespeare", "children": [{"id": "0f64d86d-3606-49c1-9fbe-9d8d38b8e434", "type": "concept", "title": "Emendation"}, {"id": "9bf9f447-5e30-4483-92f7-55880ba12eae", "type": "concept", "title": "Collation"}, {"id": "84e30737-fe4e-412f-9f8c-14a6e34996aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Glosses"}, {"id": "326fb328-2eeb-4d38-9209-7581f918519c", "type": "concept", "title": "Editorial notes"}], "description": "Gist: Modern editions make interpretive decisions about wording, punctuation, stage directions, and annotation."}, {"id": "edc06cff-7f2a-4b51-91a4-cb491a644f35", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Print and regulation", "children": [{"id": "3a0d90ba-3886-4732-a381-4b1cd1eea43f", "type": "concept", "title": "Stationers' Company"}, {"id": "d670018a-fa65-4d45-b51e-f229e46f94a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Master of the Revels"}, {"id": "594af9ce-a651-4b68-8a51-ad06865d0e49", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage"}, {"id": "3dbd3048-123e-4e38-96fe-179315e54afa", "type": "concept", "title": "Book trade"}], "description": "Gist: Theater and print were shaped by censorship, licensing, patronage, and market demand."}]}, {"id": "7547aa7b-a106-4546-9e5f-b044dd9be5a3", "type": "topic", "title": "Adaptation, Reception, and Global Shakespeare", "description": "Follow Shakespeare across performance history, film, translation, education, empire, popular culture, and global adaptation.", "children": [{"id": "8a13bf90-2e43-4249-b8fc-e56c51c2c952", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Performance history", "children": [{"id": "897c2b2c-42da-45d5-81d2-6e22840d1e58", "type": "concept", "title": "Restoration adaptations"}, {"id": "6b81d2f3-0994-402a-9493-188d064660b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Victorian Shakespeare"}, {"id": "37e857fd-e803-424b-977a-50d7def98b83", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern dress"}, {"id": "9dcc3054-3138-4ea1-b631-66df064f17e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Directorial concepts"}], "description": "Gist: Every era remakes Shakespeare through acting styles, cuts, design, politics, and audience expectations."}, {"id": "31d902dc-f792-444f-b468-f63c2897d1c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Film and media", "children": [{"id": "2604c294-edd5-4e9d-b6dd-7505789ba053", "type": "concept", "title": "Close-up"}, {"id": "8f8bc6f4-00d9-4881-a71f-a69340961fc3", "type": "concept", "title": "Montage"}, {"id": "c5c2d812-b5e3-454e-96ad-dfeb992290fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Location shooting"}, {"id": "9953f9a1-e2d8-479e-b315-4a9136f8ef20", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound design"}], "description": "Gist: Screen Shakespeare changes scale, camera, sound, editing, and spectatorship."}, {"id": "8fc81c36-bb03-428b-bfd7-6b8c1c480420", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Shakespeare", "children": [{"id": "0a7de5be-f9ac-4672-86df-be309d26c0c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation"}, {"id": "7c16767c-3eb0-40ce-9abf-56522513b84a", "type": "concept", "title": "Postcolonial adaptation"}, {"id": "5af8def0-0282-405b-b3ba-88b79887e0af", "type": "concept", "title": "Education"}, {"id": "297d1030-43d2-4c64-9cf0-8dcd153de559", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular culture"}], "description": "Gist: Translation and adaptation show Shakespeare as a world author shaped by local histories."}]}, {"id": "ec883ba2-25a1-44cc-92d1-cd97ed3772ec", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Methods and Research Practices", "description": "Develop close reading, performance analysis, historicist research, source work, scholarly argument, and MLA documentation.", "children": [{"id": "15cc039a-1cb1-4eec-a820-4466f35bf6af", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close reading and performance analysis", "children": [{"id": "9de0e702-a20e-4c13-a3ed-87dce3f5f460", "type": "concept", "title": "Passage analysis"}, {"id": "9f479781-e6d3-402a-b763-517b855acfb3", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene analysis"}, {"id": "0767ab94-dc1d-4baa-b384-1d16fbdc8539", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance choices"}, {"id": "3c277b34-4a15-48a9-9ddb-eadb88bccd14", "type": "concept", "title": "Dramatic structure"}], "description": "Gist: Students combine textual detail with staging awareness."}, {"id": "cab4418c-4ff2-47d8-ae90-cf2d5dd51ae0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historicist research", "children": [{"id": "6d68f88a-fa8e-488a-b5a3-7bee5b250139", "type": "concept", "title": "EEBO"}, {"id": "c76db70f-1ae6-435d-a63c-4022a04f53b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Folger resources"}, {"id": "33ec7df5-e6e8-4ef4-9e1c-03237bb0ed22", "type": "concept", "title": "Shakespeare Documented"}, {"id": "36303134-2767-45cc-9304-ba6b443e8baa", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly editions"}], "description": "Gist: Research connects plays to archives, sources, institutions, and cultural debates."}, {"id": "9f17535c-8cd1-4b64-ad30-1f794a1717db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scholarly argument", "children": [{"id": "9812d41f-d6fa-4559-8a5f-02d0d305e191", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question"}, {"id": "59cabc77-3ff7-4276-b7b4-97955aa106cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Secondary sources"}, {"id": "9e360877-8bf0-41a8-89c7-76518fb6b5d4", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA citation"}, {"id": "d055043a-5e26-4456-9baf-34fb043a81c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision"}], "description": "Gist: Students practice joining evidence, criticism, and an arguable claim."}]}, {"id": "fe00b20b-c84a-42ce-a87c-aec63e8b2c91", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Close Reading, Performance, and Historicist Argument", "description": "Synthesize textual analysis, performance insight, context, criticism, and revision in a final Shakespeare project.", "children": [{"id": "11845786-dd74-4366-85fe-09efba8da7af", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project design", "children": [{"id": "3af6f58b-91a3-4cb7-bfd7-c51781929986", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic scope"}, {"id": "738f3cbe-4c35-4a95-83bf-f5ae19839dcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary text selection"}, {"id": "b7f9dbee-88bc-462e-bd6a-4716e39ae996", "type": "concept", "title": "Research trail"}, {"id": "a2c022b1-6966-42c6-a3b1-ea01e8ee2049", "type": "concept", "title": "Working thesis"}], "description": "Gist: Students choose a focused question that can support real textual and contextual analysis."}, {"id": "9d7723ce-57c6-48c2-a72b-071cea8f6baa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence integration", "children": [{"id": "5a7f0318-368e-4bbc-9175-241014eddc7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation framing"}, {"id": "5aec4288-01fa-496b-901b-d4a8b9310cad", "type": "concept", "title": "Stage evidence"}, {"id": "7b22d7cd-2a2e-4631-972d-90aaada49cab", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical source"}, {"id": "a0d01fe0-b209-42be-bc70-66255c0b82d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical conversation"}], "description": "Gist: The capstone requires close reading, performance insight, history, and criticism to work together."}, {"id": "f1233564-2b4e-4ae0-a50f-b907ed2b92b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Presentation and revision", "children": [{"id": "91c1df13-f349-48e9-9ea8-3d5b3ccb4fbb", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer workshop"}, {"id": "23819d42-e1b6-48fc-adfb-700e5826df04", "type": "concept", "title": "Reverse outline"}, {"id": "61b0b2aa-19e8-4135-bd3b-54cc2a03cbb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral argument"}, {"id": "4e983069-a4b8-468b-b3a6-c8cadb852e07", "type": "concept", "title": "Final reflection"}], "description": "Gist: Students refine the project through peer response, oral presentation, and careful final revision."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$shakjson$::jsonb,
